{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"DOI:10.1038/s41587-026-03307-w","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"42823510","source":"MED","pmid":"42823510","doi":"10.1038/s41587-026-03307-w","title":"Deep learning perturbation models can outperform baselines on calibrated metrics.","authorString":"Miller HE, Mejia GM, Leblanc FJA, Swain B, Wang B, de Lima Camillo LP.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Miller HE","lastName":"Miller","firstName":"Henry E","initials":"HE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Shift Bioscience Ltd., Cambridge, UK. henry@shiftbioscience.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Mejia GM","lastName":"Mejia","firstName":"Gabriel M","initials":"GM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Shift Bioscience Ltd., Cambridge, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Leblanc FJA","lastName":"Leblanc","firstName":"Francis J A","initials":"FJA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Shift Bioscience Ltd., Cambridge, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Swain B","lastName":"Swain","firstName":"Brendan","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9924-9284"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Shift Bioscience Ltd., Cambridge, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang B","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Bo","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9620-3413"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Computer Science, University of Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Peter Munk Cardiac Centre, University Health Network, Toronto, Ontario, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Department of Laboratory Medicine and Pathobiology, University of Toronto, Toronto, Ontario, Canada."},{"affiliation":"Vector Institute, Toronto, Ontario, Canada."}]}},{"fullName":"de Lima Camillo LP","lastName":"de Lima Camillo","firstName":"Lucas Paulo","initials":"LP","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8166-0761"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Shift Bioscience Ltd., Cambridge, UK."},{"affiliation":"School of Clinical Medicine, University of Cambridge, Cambridge, UK."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8166-0761"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9924-9284"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9620-3413"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4276356,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Nat Biotechnol","title":"Nature biotechnology","essn":"1546-1696","issn":"1087-0156","nlmid":"9604648","isoabbreviation":"Nat Biotechnol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Recent benchmarks report that deep-learning-based genetic perturbation models fail to outperform uninformative baselines. Here we introduce a positive control baseline and metric calibration framework, showing-across 14 datasets and 18 metrics-that common benchmarking metrics (for example, mean squared error and Pearson Δ) are frequently miscalibrated, with reduced sensitivity to measure positive model performance. Under well-calibrated metrics, we find that deep-learning-based genetic perturbation models can outperform uninformative baselines.","affiliation":"Shift Bioscience Ltd., Cambridge, UK. henry@shiftbioscience.com.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41587-026-03307-w"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLink